WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
 So the last topic we'll talk about here in object orientation

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.320
 is the notion of inheritance.

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.760
 And this is a form of code reuse,

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.200
 and it's one of the more advanced aspects

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.800
 of object-oriented programming.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.640
 So just kind of understand what it is at a high level,

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.400
 and then you know where to come back to

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.960
 when you need to learn a bit more about inheritance.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:23.800
 So the idea is instead of making a new class from scratch,

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.200
 we actually make a new class

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.760
 by starting with an existing class.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.080
 We are extending it,

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.080
 or another word for this is subclassing.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.000
 And it's sort of a situation where you're like,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.280
 I've got this code, and I've got this data,

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:38.440
 and I just need to add a few things to it,

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.640
 and then I'll have a whole new thing.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.640
 And as you design objects and what we call object hierarchies,

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:45.720
 you often do this.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.960
 And it's a form of sort of real clever code reuse.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.880
 But again, don't necessarily think

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:54.960
 that you're supposed to know when to use this,

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.560
 or why to use this.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.200
 Right now, it's just terminology, okay, just terminology.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.720
 We have what call these as parent-child relationships.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.360
 The original class is called a parent,

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:07.320
 and the new class is called the child class.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.520
 So subclasses are another word for this.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.600
 You have a class, and then you subclass it.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.160
 I think extending and inheriting and parent-child

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:19.160
 are probably better ways of expressing it than subclassing.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:20.640
 So here's a bit of code.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.560
 Let's take a look at this.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.400
 This code's unchanged.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.440
 It's the party animal code that we've been saying all along.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.720
 It's the one that we construct and put a name in.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.760
 And now what we're going to do is extend it.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.720
 And so you'll notice that this code down here

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.720
 is the part that's doing the extending.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.520
 So we're making a new class, football fan,

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.840
 and by putting in parentheses before the colon,

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.240
 party animal, that says football fan

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.160
 inherits everything that is party animal,

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:50.520
 meaning the X, the name, the init, the party,

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.640
 all those methods and data are sitting there.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.520
 And now we're going to add a new variable.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.200
 So football fan has, in addition to all those other variables,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.320
 has points, and it has a touchdown method.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.920
 And we add seven of the points, and then we call the party.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:05.920
 And that does that.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.440
 So this is calling this method because football fan

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:12.840
 includes X, name, and party, and init, and everything,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.680
 and all this stuff, this constructor.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:18.680
 So this football fan is really an amalgamation

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.240
 of all these things together.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.240
 Party animal is just this stuff, right?

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:25.720
 And so we still have two classes.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:26.800
 We don't just have one.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.920
 We didn't erase the party animal class.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.440
 And so we take a look at the code that we can run here.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.520
 We can say, oh, okay, let's make a party animal, Sally.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.280
 And so that constructs an object like this

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:43.200
 and then stores that in S, and with an X starting out at zero.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.160
 And then we call S party, oops, better change that color.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.480
 Starts out at zero, and then we call the party method,

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.480
 and that changes it to one, okay?

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.000
 And so this bit of code, it's as if this part

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.600
 doesn't matter at all because it is a party animal.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:01.000
 It's not a football fan.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.640
 But now if we take a look at this code down here,

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:10.000
 take this code down here, we're going to construct

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.800
 a football fan and pass in gym.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.320
 So that actually uses the underscore init from party animal

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.720
 because we extended party animal to make football fans.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.480
 So we inherited all of the good that was in there.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:25.160
 So there it's going to make a name, a variable X,

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:26.720
 which is going to start at zero,

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.640
 a variable name that's going to have gym in it,

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.000
 and a variable points that's going to have a zero in it.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.600
 So this J variable has more things in it

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:37.440
 than the S variable has.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.780
 And so we can call the J party.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:45.320
 And if we call J party, that goes here and adds one to X.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:47.440
 Right, so that adds one to X.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:49.080
 And then we call J touchdown.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:54.080
 Well that comes down in here and adds seven to the points.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:56.720
 Right, and then calls party within us.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.280
 And so self.party is the current object,

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:03.320
 i.e. self and J are the same thing, right?

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.720
 Self.party, and then it goes up here and passes self in

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:09.720
 and it adds one to the X.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.040
 In this case, of this J variable.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.280
 So this becomes two.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.280
 And that's where it prints out seven and two,

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:19.240
 and away you go.

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:23.440
 And so it's a way for you to kind of take all this stuff

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:27.720
 and stuff it into a class by making a new class

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.440
 and just add the extending bits,

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:32.600
 the bits that are in addition to the other stuff.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:36.520
 So like I said, inheritance is a powerful

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:37.520
 and wonderful concept.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:41.160
 It's a form of, excellent form of reuse.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:46.160
 But basically the whole purpose of this lecture

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.960
 was so that I could in the future just use these words

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.760
 and you would understand them as compared to,

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:52.720
 I just want to say method.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:53.960
 And I've been saying method all along

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:55.960
 in this high time that I defined it.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.640
 So let's just review one last time.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:00.600
 Class is a template.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:04.160
 It is not actually a thing, it is a shape of a thing.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.960
 And we define it and say when we make one of these things,

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:08.320
 it's going to have these variables in it,

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.360
 it's going to have these methods in it.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:13.000
 Attributes, variables within a class.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.480
 Method is a function that's inside of a class.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:18.760
 Object is once we construct a class,

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.240
 we get back an object.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:25.240
 And so object here is the snowman cookies.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:28.200
 Class is the snowman cookie cutter.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:33.160
 And a constructor is a bit of code that sets up our object,

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:36.920
 our instance, when it first is created.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:40.600
 And inheritance is this ability to create a new class

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:44.240
 but take all and import and affect all the capabilities

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:46.360
 of an existing class.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:49.860
 So object-oriented is awesome.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.720
 For the rest of this class,

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:53.240
 we're not going to write any object code,

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:54.800
 we're not going to use class at all,

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:55.960
 but we're going to use objects.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:57.400
 And literally you've been using objects

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:59.080
 from the beginning of this course.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:03.320
 As soon as you said print, whoops,

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:08.320
 as soon as you said x equals high, that's an object.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:11.020
 And as soon as you said x.upper,

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:14.280
 you were calling a method, right?

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.400
 You've been calling a method all along.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:20.460
 When you're doing something like fh equals open,

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:24.580
 this thing you're getting back, that's an object.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:28.000
 And then you do fh.read or whatever,

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:29.960
 you're calling a method in the dot operator.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:32.160
 So you've been using objects all along.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:34.720
 I now am just finally explaining to you

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:38.160
 when I say call the read method or call the upper method

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.680
 or what's this little dot and why is that there?

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:44.040
 So again, it's time for us to understand that

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:46.880
 but it will take you a long time

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:49.720
 before you encounter a problem that's large enough

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:52.120
 where as part of your solution,

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:53.840
 you're going to make a new object.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:55.880
 But when you do, it's really a powerful thing.

00:06:55.880 --> 00:07:00.040
 I mean, it's a really bad idea for me as a teacher to say,

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:01.240
 oh, write a bunch of objects.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:03.360
 It's like, it's premature for that.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.840
 It's later is when you will actually learn

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:08.200
 how to use objects and you'll be like,

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:11.960
 oh, thank heaven that these objects are here, okay?

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:14.320
 So, that's all for now.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:15.160
 Thanks for listening.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:16.080
 See you on the net.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.660
 (gentle music)

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:33.820
 (gentle music)

